WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.703
And out of this world scientific sample

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:07.640
seven years in the making

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:08.942
touchdown.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:12.245
I repeat he has touchdown.

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:14.614
For the first time in U.S.

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:15.782
history, NASA

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:18.118
is returning an asteroid sample

00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:19.252
from space.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:21.121
CNN's Space and defense correspondent

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:21.421
Christine

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:22.689
Fisher has more on

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:24.924
this remarkable mission.

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:27.727
Well, Fred the OSIRIS-REx spacecraft left

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:29.763
earth back in 2016

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.097
Since then it has traveled

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:32.932
more than 4 billion miles

00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:33.700
to the asteroid

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:34.801
Bennu and back

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:36.002
before finally

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.370
releasing a capsule

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.606
over the United States on Sunday.

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:40.240
Morning

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.341
and inside

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:41.941
this capsule

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:42.609
was about

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:45.145
half a pound worth of rocks and dust

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:47.013
and soil from the surface

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:48.782
of the asteroid itself.

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:50.550
And this is really the first time

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.684
that NASA has ever done

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:52.952
anything like this.

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:54.421
This capsule

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:55.989
entered the Earth's atmosphere, traveling

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:59.025
at about 27,000 miles per hour.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.293
It hit temperatures

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:02.629
of about 5000 degrees Fahrenheit

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:06.766
before finally landing in the Utah desert

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.835
at just about 11 miles per hour.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:11.204
Thanks to some pretty big parachutes

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:13.273
which help slowed the landing

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:14.507
from their recovery.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:16.376
Crews that had been training for years

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:17.977
for this very moment,

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:19.045
swarmed the capsule.

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.180
They were able to check it out,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.082
make sure it was still intact.

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:22.949
It was

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:24.551
they were able to make sure it was not

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:25.952
the battery was not leaking

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.487
any toxic fumes.

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:28.855
It was not

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:29.522
So from there,

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.057
they were able to safely transport it

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:31.825
to a clean room.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:32.859
And from there,

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:33.960
it's going to be going on

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.361
to the Johnson Space Center

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:36.563
in Houston, Texas,

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:37.597
where it will be opened

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.099
and revealed to the public

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:40.500
in about two weeks.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.001
And that is where scientists

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.603
are going to be spending

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:46.606
the next few years, really looking into

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:50.944
what exactly this sample has returned.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.078
And this is a sample

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:53.746
from the asteroid Bennu.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:54.914
It's a very ancient

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.117
asteroid from the beginning

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:58.118
of the time around.

00:01:58.118 --> 00:01:59.919
When the solar system was formed.

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:01.187
And so scientists are hoping

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:02.922
to gain some insight into

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.524
if this is the kind of asteroid

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:05.258
that could have

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:08.161
perhaps seeded planet Earth

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:09.329
with the elements

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:10.396
that were needed for life,

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.632
things like water and carbon.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:13.299
There was a belief

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.135
that it was these types of asteroid

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:16.903
that upon impacting

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:18.872
Earth, were able to seed it

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:20.273
with those elements,

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:21.174
give it the elements

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:23.309
that were needed to create life here.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.777
So scientists are going to be

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:25.411
looking into that.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:27.580
They're also going to be looking at

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.349
the planetary defense side of things,

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:30.850
because there is a very,

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:31.818
very small chance

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:32.585
that this asteroid

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:33.753
could hit Earth

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:36.322
in about 150 years from now.

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:38.091
It's the small chance,

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:38.992
but it is a chance.

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:39.592
And so

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:40.960
NASA wants to study

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.263
as much about it as possible.

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:44.531
What do you think about this?

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:46.633
I mean, this is this is cool stuff.

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.268
What kind of information do we think

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:49.669
we're going to get from this space rock?

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.306
Well, it's nothing less than perhaps

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:54.841
unlocking the key to

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.610
how life got started on this planet, Jim.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.212
So big stuff.

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:01.147
You could imagine this planet

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:03.349
about 4 billion years ago

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.552
not being such a cushy place for life,

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:07.387
mostly magma.

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:10.190
But as it cooled off, a lot of scientists

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.758
believe these days

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:14.527
that it was bombarded by asteroids

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.895
that brought perhaps

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:18.031
the building blocks of life,

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:20.233
amino acids and carbon

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.901
and some of the ingredients

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.903
that make it all possible

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.838
for us to have this conversation

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:27.440
billions of years later.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.075
What's interesting about asteroids

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:29.842
is that, you know,

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:30.677
we here on Earth

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:31.744
have gone through

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.112
all kinds of tectonic

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.547
and geologic activity.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:35.882
In the meantime,

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.684
sort of covering up all the evidence

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.252
but asteroids.

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:40.787
It's like having those ingredients

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:42.822
in a deep freeze out there in space.

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:44.257
And if you could go and grab

00:03:44.257 --> 00:03:45.458
some of those ingredients

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:46.125
and take a look,

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.361
which is what this mission did.

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:51.631
Maybe you'll solve some big questions.

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:53.233
And how did they do it?

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:53.499
I mean,

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:54.968
how did they get this rock off

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:55.768
of that asteroid?

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:56.102
If I could

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:58.238
just ask a basic question there.

00:03:58.238 --> 00:04:00.039
I'm kind of amazing. Yeah. Well, this is

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:02.375
kind of cool.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:04.711
The short answer is very carefully.

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.512
It's a seven year mission.

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.014
They flew to Bennu,

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:09.882
which is a near-Earth object,

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:10.917
which incidentally,

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:11.551
will fly

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:13.052
very close to Earth

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:13.920
in the coming years

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:14.821
and could potentially

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.022
put a bull's eye on us.

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:17.423
But that's another story.

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:19.993
But the trick with ANU is

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:22.262
it's only about 600 feet across,

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:23.463
so there's really no gravity there.

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:24.931
You can't really land

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.799
in any meaningful way there.

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.001
And so they did

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:30.370
what amounts to a pogo stick style

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:33.339
bonk on on that asteroid,

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:34.140
sucked up

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.241
some of these rocks

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:36.643
and went off on their way.

00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:38.911
So the seven year mission

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:41.614
was only about a few seconds

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.650
of actually collecting the samples.

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:44.984
Interesting.

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:47.153
And it sounds like Bennu is a bad boy.

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:48.021
Is that the deal?

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:48.755
Miles, I mean,

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:50.189
we were just saying a few moments ago

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.958
that this thing might hit the Earth

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:54.394
in 100 or 200 years from now.

00:04:54.394 --> 00:04:55.428
Are we going to have to get Bruce

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:57.196
Willis up Bennu in the next

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:58.531
space and all that? Well, is.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:00.133
Yeah.

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:00.633
It's been

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:01.567
slated to fly

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.202
between the moon and Earth

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:04.771
in the next decade,

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:07.473
and depending on how that goes

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.509
in another 50 years, beyond that,

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:11.477
it could very well

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.479
have our number painted on it.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.114
And we need to watch Bennu

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:15.848
very carefully.

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:17.350
It's one of those near-Earth objects.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.451
Now, this is an asteroid

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.086
that won't cause a dinosaur

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:23.589
like extinction event, but it would.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:24.590
It's about the size

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:25.858
of the Empire State Building

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.360
and would cause a huge

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.861
regional catastrophe.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.330
So it's important

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.066
that NASA keep a close eye on Bennu,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.234
and we have the technology.

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:35.635
Do we,

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:38.071
do we not, to potentially deal with that?

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:41.140
I suppose in 100 or 200 years, we damn

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:41.541
well better

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:43.509
have the technology to deal with it

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:44.377
if we got to do something

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:45.278
to that asteroid.

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:46.279
We've just experimented

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:47.714
with knocking these things off course

00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:49.749
yeah.

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:50.383
Just about a year

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:51.918
ago, almost exactly a year ago,

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:53.986
this DART mission proved that we could,

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:56.889
you know, auger into an asteroid

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.091
and perturb its orbit.

00:05:58.091 --> 00:05:59.425
Ever so slightly,

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:00.493
but just enough

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:01.027
if you do it

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:02.762
far enough in advance

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:06.432
to make a just a spectacular

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:06.966
light show

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:09.836
as opposed to a regional catastrophe.

00:06:10.837 --> 00:06:11.971
Very interesting. So.

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:12.438
All right, Miles.

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:14.173
Well, we knew you were the perfect person

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:15.074
to go to on this.

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:16.242
We appreciate it very much.

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:17.110
Great to see, as always.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.211
Thanks, Miles.

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:20.146
You're welcome, Jim.

